{
  "rules": [
    "Systematic Underestimation of Bullish Spikes: The model consistently fails to capture upward momentum, forecasting flat or slightly declining paths while true prices surge (e.g., +10-15 EUR jumps in Jan-April). Apply a positive momentum bias when recent history shows consecutive daily increases.",
    "Excessive Mean Reversion Lag: Forecasts revert to the historical mean too quickly after price spikes, missing the sustained high-level plateau. Delay the mean reversion adjustment by 5-7 days following a breakout above the 20-day moving average.",
    "Sentiment Ignorance: Neutral sentiment (0.00) is treated as a baseline for stability, but the model ignores the predictive value of prior positive sentiment shifts (e.g., 0.25) which precede significant rallies. Weight recent positive sentiment shifts more heavily to initiate upward trend adjustments.",
    "Horizon-Specific Overshoot Correction: The model slightly overestimates prices in the very short term (next 1-3 days) immediately following a drop, then underestimates the subsequent recovery. Reduce the initial forecast slope by 10% after a sharp decline to avoid premature recovery assumptions."
  ]
}
